@@s034 How to read the atlas
@@s035 Every animation is a <b>sheet</b>: the steps are written one under the other, as on paper. Press <b>Next →</b> (or the arrows <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> after clicking the sheet). <b>All steps</b> shows them together for review.
@@s036 The colours always have the same meaning, on every page:
@@s037 Try it: what is abs(-42)?
@@s038 The starting datum: I call <code>abs</code> with <code>-42</code>.
@@s039 Scala replaces <code>n</code> with <code>-42</code> inside the body.
@@s040 <code>-42 &lt; 0</code> is true. The <code>else</code> branch is no longer needed: I discard it.
@@s041 What remains is <code>-(-42)</code>, that is the result.
@@s042 At the bottom of every page there is <b>Mark this page as understood</b>: the tick appears in the index and the count at the bottom left tells you how much is left.
@@s043 Map of the topics
@@s044 For the exam
@@s045 What you must be able to do at the end
@@s046 From the official course description, rewritten in plain words:
@@s047 Design, test and run functional programs in Scala.
@@s048 Use types to describe libraries: polymorphism, type-level functions, higher-order types.
@@s049 Recognise <b>monads</b> (structures with <code>map</code> and <code>flatMap</code>), use them and design new ones.
@@s050 Reason about <b>eager</b> (immediate) and <b>lazy</b> (when needed) evaluation: advantages and disadvantages.
@@s051 Compare APIs in pure style and in stateful style.
@@s052 Implement solutions starting from research papers (for example lenses).
@@s053 Estimated time: 50% homework, 20% preparation, 10% lectures, 10% exercises, 10% exam.
